  "response": "**VERDICT: REJECT**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component fails core accessibility requirements for form labeling and error state communication. A visual label exists as a disconnected `<div>`, and error messages are rendered visually but never associated with the input programmatically. Screen reader users will hear \"Choose File\" without context, and upon validation failure, will hear an error message with no way to link it back to the field that triggered it.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1. Missing `<label>` element; text is in a generic `<div>` instead of a labeled control.\n2. Error state (`setError`) updates React state but does not update `aria-invalid` or `aria-describedby`.\n3. Screen reader will announce the default button name (\"Choose File\") rather than the intended action (\"Upload file\").\n4. Focus order is functional (Tab reaches input), but state announcements are absent.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Missing accessible label for file input**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyFileInput.jsx:18` renders `<div>Upload file</div>` instead of a `<label>`. The `<input>` at line 20 has no associated text. axe-core rule `label` fired (impact: critical) against this selector.\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users.\n   - **WCAG/APG**: WCAG 1.3.1 Info and Relationships; WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: The input must have a programmatically associated label (e.g., `<label htmlFor=\"file-input\">Upload file</label>`).\n   - **Fix**: Replace the generic `<div>Upload file</div>` with a `<label htmlFor={inputId}>Upload file</label>` and add `id={inputId}` to the `<input>`.\n\n2. **Error message not associated with input**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/BuggyFileInput.jsx:26-29` renders error text in a `<div className=\"error-message\">` but provides no `id` for it, and the input lacks `aria-describedby`. The trace shows `invalid: false` even when `setError('File too large')` is active.\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users.\n   - **WCAG/APG**: WCAG 3.3.1 Error Identification; WCAG 4.1.3 Status Messages.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: The error message must be linked to the input via `aria-describedby`, and the input must reflect the invalid state via `aria-invalid=\"true\"`.\n   - **Fix**: Add `id=\"file-error\"` to the error `<div>`. Add `aria-describedby={error ? \"file-error\" : undefined}` and `aria-invalid={!!error}` to the `<input>`.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Unlabeled default button name vs. visual context mismatch**\n   - **Evidence**: Trace `step_0001` shows `ax_name_role_state.name = \"Choose File\"`. Census entry index 2 shows spoken phrase \"Upload file\" with `tag: null, selector: null`, confirming the visual text is read as an orphaned phrase.\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG/APG**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: The accessible name should clearly describe the action (\"Upload file\") and be programmatically tied to the control.\n   - **Fix**: Use `<label htmlFor=\"file-input\">Upload file</label>` so the accessible name becomes \"Upload file\" instead of the browser default \"Choose File\".\n\n2. **Error state not communicated to assistive technology**\n   - **Evidence**: `handleFileChange` sets React state `error`, but no ARIA attributes update. Trace shows `states: {\"invalid\":\"false\",...}` at both step 1 and step 3, despite the visual UI updating.\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG/APG**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value.\n   - **Expected Behavior**: `aria-invalid` must toggle to `\"true\"` when validation fails.\n   - **Fix**: Update the input's `aria-invalid` prop dynamically: `aria-invalid={error ? \"true\" : false}`.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Page-level landmark/heading gaps**: axe-core fired `landmark-one-main`, `page-has-heading-one`, and `region` violations. While these are container/page issues, they degrade the component's usability when embedded in a larger app. Add `<main>` wrapping and an `<h1>` or `<h2>` for the upload section.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- **Error message live region**: Consider adding `role=\"alert\"` to the error `<div>` so it announces immediately upon validation failure, rather than requiring the user to tab back to the input to hear `aria-invalid`.\n- **File type hinting**: The `accept` attribute is present (good), but a visible helper text explaining file size/type limits should be associated via `aria-describedby` alongside the error message.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- **Programmatic label association**: No `<label>` element exists. The visual text is structurally invisible to AT.\n- **Error state synchronization**: React state updates are not mirrored to ARIA attributes (`aria-invalid`, `aria-describedby`).\n- **Live announcement for errors**: No `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live=\"assertive\"` on the error container.\n- **Helper text association**: File restrictions (`.pdf,.doc,.docx`, 5MB limit) are not communicated to AT beyond the `accept` attribute.\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n- **Screen reader user**: Hears \"Upload file\" (orphaned div), then \"Choose File, button\". Upon selecting a large file, hears \"File too large\" but has no programmatic link to the input. The error is visually clear but AT-blind.\n- **Keyboard-only user**: Tab reaches the input correctly (`focus_moved: true` in trace). No traps. Can activate via Enter/Space. Focus order is logical.\n- **Low vision user**: Visual layout works, but reliance on color (if any) for errors isn't shown here. The missing label means zooming doesn't help discoverability.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**: Error message appears below the input visually, which is good, but without `aria-describedby`, screen reader users get no contextual link. Instructions are only visual.\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component fails WCAG 1.3.1 and 4.1.2 at a CRITICAL level due to the missing label. It fails WCAG 3.3.1 and 4.1.3 at a MAJOR level due to unassociated error states. These are not preference issues; they block screen reader users from understanding what the input is for and which field failed validation. The code must be revised to use native `<label>` semantics and proper ARIA state management before shipping.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the parent application provide a global landmark structure that mitigates the `landmark-one-main` / `region` axe violations?\n- Is there a custom file picker UI replacing the native input elsewhere in the codebase that might require ARIA menu/listbox patterns instead of this native `<input>`?",
